---

Ardo is a static site generator for React projects. Guides in Markdown and MDX, an API reference
read from your TypeScript, and search, all prerendered at build time by Rust engines. Every page
ships as plain HTML that any static host can serve.

## The easy way in

Hand this sentence to your coding agent, in a fresh repository or one you already have:

```text
Set up Ardo in this repository and follow the official instructions.
```

It finds Ardo's [Agent Instructions](https://ardo-docs.dev/v6/guide/agent-instructions), adapts the
tested [Basic example](./examples/basic), and tells you how typecheck, tests, and build went. You
review the result like any other pull request. Prefer to do it by hand? Follow the
[manual installation](https://ardo-docs.dev/v6/guide/getting-started#manual-installation).

## How it works

1. **Write it.** Markdown and MDX guides, your own React components and layouts, API reference from
   TypeScript, and typed content collections.
2. **Bake it.** Rust engines compile your Markdown and color every code block, TypeScript 7 reads
   your types, and Vite bundles the rest. Search index, sitemap, and `llms.txt` come out of the same
   oven.
3. **Serve it.** Every page is complete HTML. Search engines see everything, readers get the content
   on the first request, and any static host will do.

A whole site starts with one line in `app/root.tsx`:

```tsx
import { ArdoRoot } from "ardo/ui"

export default function Root() {
  return <ArdoRoot />
}
```

That renders a header with search, a sidebar built from your folders, dark mode, and a footer.
[Theming](https://ardo-docs.dev/v6/guide/custom-theme) starts with three hues in `ardo.config.ts`.

## Is Ardo your kind of bird?

A great fit if:

- your product or library is built with React and TypeScript;
- docs should live in your repository and go through review;
- you want prerendered pages and little work in the browser.

Maybe not if:

- you want prose only, without React (try Starlight or VitePress);
- you need hosted editing and analytics (a hosted platform does that).

See [how Ardo compares](https://ardo-docs.dev/v6/guide/what-is-ardo#how-ardo-compares) to
Docusaurus, Starlight, VitePress, Nextra, Fumadocs, and Mintlify.

## Under the hood

Ardo stands on [Vite](https://vite.dev/), [TypeScript 7](https://www.typescriptlang.org/), and
[Oxc](https://oxc.rs/). Two engines come from our own workshop, written in Rust:
[Ferromark](https://ferromark.dev/) compiles Markdown and MDX, and [Ferriki](https://ferriki.dev/)
highlights every code block at build time.

## Package

[**ardo**](./packages/ardo) is the one package: CLI, Vite integration, runtime, and theme components.
Release history lives in [packages/ardo/CHANGELOG.md](./packages/ardo/CHANGELOG.md), and
[Releases](https://ardo-docs.dev/v6/guide/releases) lists what is shipped, experimental, and planned.

## Development

```bash
git clone https://github.com/sebastian-software/ardo.git
cd ardo
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm docs:dev
```

- `pnpm test` runs the unit and integration tests.
- `pnpm typecheck` type-checks all packages.
- `pnpm docs:build` builds the documentation site.
- `pnpm test:e2e` runs the Playwright checks against the documentation site.

[CONTRIBUTING.md](./CONTRIBUTING.md) explains the workflow, the tests, and the architecture notes.

